    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 292357\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n457 PM PDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and seasonable conditions continue through tonight.\n\n* Precipitation chances and cooler temperatures return tomorrow\n  and continue through the weekend.\n\n* Cool and dry conditions for early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nSouthwest flow continues over the forecast area ahead of a Pacific\ntrough. This is resulting in near normal temperatures and partly\ncloudy skies, a pattern that is expected to continue through\ntomorrow afternoon. As the trough continues to push onshore the\nresulting moisture is expected to combine with moisture from TS\nVance, resulting in several days of soggy conditions, particularly\nin western Arizona. Showers and thunderstorms begin to the east\nearly Friday morning, with additional development further west by\nearly evening. Activity remains mainly limited to the eastern half\nof the forecast area until Saturday evening when a secondary wave\noff the California coast kicks its way onshore. Impacts are still\nsomewhat variable as they depend strongly on the path of this\nwave. The current, more aggressive track has this wave moving\nquickly and the associated moisture resulting in precipitation\nacross the forecast area, however a slower track would limit the\nwestern moisture availability and keep showers largely in Arizona.\nMountain snow remains a possibility, however snow levels aren't\nexpected to drop below the peaks until Sunday morning, by which\npoint most of the precipitation will have concluded, limiting most\nof the potential accumulation. Activity dries out by Monday as the\nsystem moves east, leaving dry conditions and slightly below\nnormal temperatures through late week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...This\nevening, winds shift to the southwest and become light, with \nincreasing clouds expected overnight. Late tonight through mid-\nmorning Friday, isolated showers are expected to develop across \nthe area, mainly remaining over the higher terrain. However, there\nis a window between 09Z-12Z when there is around 10-20% chance of\nthunderstorms to develop and move northward across the Valley. \nGusty outflow winds and SCT-BKN ceilings around 8kft can be \nexpected with any shower or thunderstorm activity. Winds remain \nlight through the morning, gradually veering to the southwest and \nbecoming gusty with gusts to around 18-20KT by early afternoon. \nLate afternoon into early evening, there is potential for another \nround of thunderstorms, but confidence in precise timing, \nlocation, and direct impacts remains low at this time. Gusts \ndiminish late evening with light winds overnight.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Clouds continue \nincreasing across the region through the forecast period, with \nceilings becoming SCT-BKN between 7-10kft by Friday morning. \nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected \nacross northwestern Arizona and southern Nevada late tonight, with\na better chance Friday late afternoon. Gusty outflow winds and \nbrief reductions in ceilings can be expected in the vicinity of \nshowers and storms. Confidence in direct impacts to area terminals\nremains low at this time, with Las Vegas Valley terminals having \nthe highest probability of seeing showers/thunderstorms in the \nvicinity. Gusty up-valley winds continue into early evening at \nKBIH in the northern Owens Valley, diminishing thereafter with 5- \n10KT winds areawide through tonight. Winds shift to the south and \nsouthwest by Friday afternoon, with gusts to around 20-25KT common\nthrough the end of the forecast period. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LKN\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
